// expect-error: NITPICK-MACRO-004
// expect-error-at: 26:1
//
// A MACRO THAT INVOKES ITSELF NEVER SETTLES, AND ITS DECLARATION SAYS SO (D-057,
// 0.6.1; DEF-172, 1.6.1e step 3).
//
// A FIXED-POINT LOOP MUST TERMINATE. Nothing in the prototype's corpus bounds it,
// so a macro that invokes itself iterates forever there — unacceptable in a
// compiler under formal verification, where termination is itself a property to
// establish.
//
// UNTIL 1.6.1e STEP 3 THE ROUNDS FOUND THIS: they ran to their bound and the
// refusal landed at module 0's root, which is the PRELUDE's first line, in a
// sentence that named no macro. It is decided at the declaration now -- a body has
// no conditional, so a macro whose body writes `#itself()` reaches itself at every
// instantiation -- and reported there, with the chain: "macro `self_recursive`
// never settles: `self_recursive` invokes itself". The program is not expanded
// after it.
//
// THE BODY EMITS A DECLARATION AS WELL AS THE INVOCATION, so that it is a
// declaration body standing where a declaration body belongs: the one thing wrong
// with this file is the cycle. (A body of nothing but `#self_recursive();` is an
// alias of itself -- the same refusal, `cycle_uninvoked.npk`'s shape.)
mod:bounds;

macro:self_recursive = () {
    func:emitted_each_round = int32() never fails { pass 1i32; };
    #self_recursive();
};

#self_recursive();

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { exit 0i32; };
